# Copy to .env for running the app directly (uv run uvicorn, deploy/queue_worker.py).
#
# This file is read by python-dotenv, which takes values literally, so a bcrypt hash may
# contain single $ characters. The Compose stack does NOT read this file: it reads
# deploy/.env.server, where Compose substitution requires every literal $ to be doubled.
# Copy deploy/.env.server.example instead when you are deploying with Docker.
# The compose stack refuses to start while a required secret is missing.

# --- deployment mode -------------------------------------------------------
APP_ENV=production
# Exact browser origin(s), comma separated. Required in production; there is no wildcard
# fallback, because a wildcard would let any page call the authorized API.
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost

# --- authentication --------------------------------------------------------
# Either name is accepted; generate one with scripts inside app/common/secret_rotation.py.
JWT_SECRET=
AUTH_USERNAME=
# bcrypt hash of the bootstrap password, never the password itself.
AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH=

# --- storage ---------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_DB=enterprise_brain
POSTGRES_USER=enterprise_brain
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS=200
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# --- persistence backend (R256) ------------------------------------------------
# Where control-plane state lives: the artifact registry, the trace ledger, and the
# dataset registrations. Two spellings: postgres, and json.
#
# json is not a smaller deployment, it is a different durability promise. Artifacts and
# traces fall back to a sidecar file; dataset registrations fall back to an in-process
# table that is empty the moment the process restarts. Nothing is lost while the app is
# up, and everything registered since it came up is lost at the next restart -- with a
# warning in the log and no error to read.
#
# So the sample says postgres. The prerequisite for that value, bare metal included:
# a PostgreSQL that DATABASE_URL reaches, and one run of scripts/migrate.py against it
# (no subcommand; it reads DATABASE_URL or --database-url). Migration 0010 refuses to guess
# a vector width, so EMBEDDING_MODEL and EMBEDDING_DIMENSION below must be declared in the
# same file before that run, or it rolls back and names those two variables in stderr.
# README.md gives the command under 不使用 Docker 时, and app/db/migrations.py says what that
# step declares for the database. This repository has no setup.sh to run those two steps for
# you, so this file and README.md are where a bare-metal operator reads them.
#
# The default in the code is still "json", deliberately, for a machine with no database
# at all -- and it is NOT this line. Three readers hold it today:
# app/storage/persistence.py, app/storage/datasets.py and app/common/audit.py. Flipping
# it is a three-file edit, and tests/test_r256_persistence_backend_default.py refuses any
# flip that moves fewer than all three, which is also what keeps R30's rule (the page and
# the code agree on one default) from being broken by a half-flip. Leaving this line out
# is a decision, and that test names the files a decision has to touch.
PERSISTENCE_BACKEND=postgres

# --- embedding profile (R22, R90a) -----------------------------------------
# Which model produces the vectors, and how wide they are. These two lines are one profile,
# not two knobs: R22 binds every index version to model + width, and migration 0010 types the
# chunk column at exactly the width declared here.
#
# Changing the embedding model means changing the width in the same edit whenever the new
# model outputs a different number of dimensions, and then rebuilding the index
# (scripts/rebuild_index.py): vectors already stored cannot be relabelled, only rewritten.
# Migration 0010 stops with a message that says so if the two disagree with what is stored.
#
# The migrate step reads both variables and declares them for the database it migrates --
# ALTER DATABASE ... SET app.embedding_dimension / app.embedding_model, in app/db/migrations.py
# -- which is what lets a clean install get past 0010 without an operator running that command
# by hand. Leave either line out and the migration stops and names the variable that is
# missing: 0010 will not guess a vector width, and neither does the runner, so the shipped
# default of 768 is never substituted for a decision nobody made.
#
# The Compose stack reads neither this file nor .env: it reads deploy/.env.server, whose sample
# carries the same pair with the same numbers. Edit both when a machine runs both ways, or the
# host and the containers will migrate and embed under different profiles.
EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
EMBEDDING_DIMENSION=768

# --- VECTOR_DUAL_WRITE: the pgvector mirror (R58, wired up by R120) ----------
# off keeps today's behaviour exactly: app/rag/pg_store.py imports no psycopg, opens no
# connection and issues no SQL. Setting it on mirrors every vector Chroma accepts into
# chunk_vectors inside the same transaction -- either both engines hold a vector or neither
# does. Chroma stays the read path either way; only R59 changes that. This is a migration step,
# not a performance knob -- see
# docs/handoff/2026-09-17-pgvector-adoption-plan.md (P2/P3) before turning it on, and turn it
# on in deploy/.env.server for a container install. A value this file does not list is reported
# and treated as off.
VECTOR_DUAL_WRITE=off

# --- INDEX_BACKEND: which engine answers a semantic read (R59b leg, R231 env spelling) -----
# Commented out on purpose, and the commented value is today's default: a shipped sample must
# not look like a box whose read path has already been switched. Moving semantic reads onto
# PostgreSQL + PGVector is a deployment decision on a planned window -- 翻这一枚闸属业主本人，
# docs/handoff/2026-09-17-pgvector-adoption-plan.md §P4 明令 agent 不改 .env -- and what is
# still open before it is worth doing is the plan's §9.3 and §13 (as of 2026-09-28: the hot-set
# give-way latency is unmeasured, the four-part department/clearance criterion is recorded as
# 未验, and the candidate width at customer corpus size is unmeasured). This file decides none
# of those gates.
#   chroma (shipped default) -- what every semantic read answers from today. The value lives in
#     app/rag/indexing.py as INDEX_BACKEND_DEFAULT and read_backend() is the only reader: it
#     asks the environment first, accepts only chroma and pgvector, and everything else -- an
#     empty line, or a typo such as pg_vetcor -- warns and stays on the shipped engine. So a
#     misspelled switch cannot leave a half-switched box behind, it can only fail to switch it.
#   pgvector -- the production vector store; R59 is the step that moves reads onto it.
# MAKING AN EDIT TAKE EFFECT. This copy reaches a bare uv run through python-dotenv, which does
# NOT override a variable the shell already exported -- a stray INDEX_BACKEND in your shell
# therefore outranks this file, and read_backend() names that price in its own docstring. The
# Compose stack does not read this file at all -- containers take deploy/.env.server, whose
# sample carries this same key with the recreate-instead-of-rebuild note.
# INDEX_BACKEND=chroma

# --- local model (version 1 manages the machine's own Ollama) --------------
# Leave LOCAL_MODEL_NAME empty to let the platform discover a chat-capable model from the
# local registry. A name that is not installed must never be configured as a default.
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434
LOCAL_MODEL_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
LOCAL_MODEL_NAME=
LOCAL_MODEL_API_KEY=local
OLLAMA_IMAGE_TAG=latest
# MODEL_THINKING: does a request ask the local model to think before it answers?
# On the OpenAI-compatible leg (LOCAL_MODEL_BASE_URL above) this is a measurement, not a
# preference. One prompt, one container, one output cap of 1536 tokens: left alone, the model
# spent the whole budget on a hidden chain of thought and answered 0 characters with
# finish_reason=length; with the request body carrying "thinking": {"type": "disabled"} it
# answered 93 characters with finish_reason=stop. The record is §42 of
# docs/handoff/2026-09-15-backend-followup-requests.md, and MODEL_MIN_ANSWER_TOKENS below is
# calibrated for whichever of these two this line asks for.
#   disabled (default) -- put that field on every compatible-leg request this process makes
#   enabled            -- send no thinking field at all, i.e. byte-for-byte the request this
#                         product sent before R100, and with it the empty answers above
# A value that is neither word -- empty, off, TRUE, a number -- resolves to disabled and logs
# one warning; the request is still sent, and every [ModelBudget] line says which mode ran.
# Do not substitute the other spellings: top-level "think": false on the compatible body and
# options.thinking_disabled on the native body are both measured at 0 characters, so neither
# means "switched off" and neither belongs in a request this client sends.
MODEL_THINKING=disabled
# MODEL_MAX_CONCURRENCY is a safety valve, not a throughput knob: it is how many requests may
# hold the one local model at once. A caller who cannot get a slot within
# MODEL_CONCURRENCY_WAIT_SECONDS (code default 60 s; unset in both env samples) is not told to
# wait longer -- that caller is answered with the offline sentence. Measured on the shipping
# host 2026-09-25 (run8 相 2 计划 §5.3): 1 / 2 / 4 concurrent routes aggregated 38.6 / 40.6 /
# 39.9 tok/s -- a flat line, because the server is not batching (OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL unset), so
# extra slots buy waiting, not work. It stays 1 here, and the window arithmetic below divides
# the queue by exactly this number.
MODEL_MAX_CONCURRENCY=1
MODEL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=120

# --- per-tier output caps and clocks (R30) --------------------------------
# MODEL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT above is the ceiling; these are what fill the space under it.
# Every local-model call belongs to one tier (app/agents/contracts.py:ModelTier), and
# every tier states how much it may write. The clock is then derived, not guessed:
#   prefill = prompt_tokens / MODEL_PREFILL_TOKENS_PER_SECOND
#   decode  = max_tokens    / MODEL_DECODE_TOKENS_PER_SECOND
#   read    = margin * (prefill + decode), clamped between the floor below and
#             MODEL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT, which stays the ceiling for one request.
# The numbers below ARE the code defaults, so deleting a line changes nothing. Raise
# the two rates on a faster machine; lower the caps if a tier keeps hitting the ceiling.
# A tier cap is what the tier asks to write; it is NOT a promise the clock can pay for.
# When the two contradict each other the request is shortened to what this ceiling and
# these rates can actually write -- see MODEL_MIN_ANSWER_TOKENS for the limit of that.
MODEL_TIER_CHAT_MAX_TOKENS=256
MODEL_TIER_PLAN_MAX_TOKENS=256
MODEL_TIER_REWRITE_MAX_TOKENS=256
MODEL_TIER_ALERT_MAX_TOKENS=384
MODEL_TIER_COMPRESS_MAX_TOKENS=512
MODEL_TIER_CODE_MAX_TOKENS=512
MODEL_TIER_ANALYSIS_MAX_TOKENS=1536
# CALIBRATED MEASUREMENTS, NOT PREFERENCES. These two rates are a measurement of one
# machine, and the only command that produces them is:
#     python scripts/bench_model_throughput.py
# Measured on the delivery baseline (CPU-only qwen3.5:9b through Ollama, 2026-09-16):
# 35.2 prefill / 8.18 decode tokens per second, rounded down -- an optimistic rate is
# exactly the cut-off-mid-answer bug these budgets exist to remove. Re-measure on the
# machine you ship and write the numbers it prints here; do not adjust them to make a
# timeout go away. As of 2026-09-19 these constants are still the CPU-only floor while
# the shipping container answers at ~37 tok/s on GPU, which is why the analysis tier is
# reported as budget_unaffordable rather than being silently clamped (R99).
MODEL_PREFILL_TOKENS_PER_SECOND=35
MODEL_DECODE_TOKENS_PER_SECOND=8
# The output cap an answer is never shortened below. Re-calibrated by R100 for the link that
# ships now: the previous value, 1537, measured a model that was THINKING -- in the shipping
# container (qwen3.5:9b, 100% GPU) num_predict 1024 and 1536 both answered 0 characters with
# done_reason=length while 4096 produced 439, so the floor sat somewhere in (1536, 4096] and
# that default took its low end plus one. MODEL_THINKING above changes what is being measured,
# so the number has to move with it: with thinking disabled on the compatible leg, 1536 tokens
# answered 93 characters and reached finish_reason=stop after 1328 billed tokens (§42 row #6).
# 1536 is therefore the smallest cap with a recorded non-empty answer on the request this
# client sends, and it is deliberately not lower -- nothing under it has been measured in this
# spelling, and §42 records that disabling thinking moves the reasoning out of the answer
# without saving a billed token, so there is no evidence the floor collapsed.
# It stays a measurement floor: an answer is never shortened past it, and a tier that cannot
# fit under it is reported (budget_verdict=budget_unaffordable) rather than answered with
# nothing. Recalibrate with the same command as the two rates above; until that script's
# compatible arm can send "thinking": {"type": "disabled"}, it can only re-measure the old
# thinking-on floor, so anything below 1536 here is 待真机标定.
# This is NOT a lever for fitting more material into the window (R255). The refusal at
# MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS below subtracts the tier's output cap, not this floor, so lowering it
# fits not one more prompt token -- it only moves the number an answer may never be shortened
# under, into the range where this model has been measured to answer with nothing at all.
MODEL_MIN_ANSWER_TOKENS=1536
MODEL_TIMEOUT_MARGIN=1.15
MODEL_TIMEOUT_FLOOR_SECONDS=10
MODEL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# n_ctx of the local server. A prompt that cannot fit this window together with its
# tier's max_tokens is refused before it is sent, with error_code=context_limit_exceeded,
# rather than answered with a completion that stops halfway and still looks like an answer.
#
# READ THIS AS A CLAIM ABOUT THE SERVER, NOT AS A KNOB THAT RESIZES IT. The window a request
# is served in is the model server's own runtime configuration, and this product never sends
# num_ctx in a request payload, so the two numbers are one decision written twice. Card size
# is not that decision: an A100 80G loaded at the server default answers inside the same 4096
# and the same prompt is still refused here. Declaring 8192 while the server holds 4096 is
# worse than today -- the guard lets the request out, the server returns HTTP 400 "exceeds the
# available context size" (measured: docs/perf/raw/rate_prefill.jsonl), and a refusal that cost
# nothing becomes a round trip that costs everyone queued behind it.
#
# THE CONVERSION -- four numbers, derived in one place
# (app/common/model_budget.py:window_plan; published on GET /api/v1/model-budget/facts as the
# "window_plan" block of the budget readout, and on every refusal log line as n_ctx= /
# full_window_seconds= / max_coherent_n_ctx= / window_coherent=):
#
#   prompt room        = MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS - tier max_tokens
#   worst-case seconds = MODEL_TIMEOUT_MARGIN * ( prompt room / MODEL_PREFILL_TOKENS_PER_SECOND
#                            + tier max_tokens / MODEL_DECODE_TOKENS_PER_SECOND )
#   a coherent window pays those seconds out of BOTH of:
#       MODEL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT                              (one request's ceiling), and
#       MODEL_CONCURRENCY_WAIT_SECONDS / MODEL_MAX_CONCURRENCY
#           -- at 1 slot the caller behind you waits a whole worst case, and past 60 s they
#              get the offline sentence rather than an answer that arrives late.
#
# Inverted for the knob an operator actually wants to move:
#   largest coherent MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS = tier max_tokens
#       + MODEL_PREFILL_TOKENS_PER_SECOND * ( min(MODEL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT,
#                                                 MODEL_CONCURRENCY_WAIT_SECONDS / slots) / margin
#                                             - tier max_tokens / MODEL_DECODE_TOKENS_PER_SECOND )
#
# Worked at the analysis cap of 1536 on both rate pairs measured on this class of host:
#   the CPU-only defaults written above (35 / 8 tok/s): this tier's own answer costs 192 s,
#     more than either budget, so NO window is coherent -- the rates are the head that has to
#     move first, and a stock install refuses on arithmetic rather than on a prompt.
#   the rates measured 2026-09-25 in the shipping container (prefill 1200, decode 40 tok/s,
#     run8 相 2 计划 §5): 4096 costs 46.6 s at a full window, 16384 costs 58.4 s and is
#     coherent, 18064 is the largest window a 60 s queue at 1 slot still pays for, and 32768
#     costs 74.1 s -- red on the queue while the 120 s clock is still comfortable.
# Raising the window without paying for it buys nothing: the request is refused one guard
# later, with error_code=task_timeout.
#
# MAKING AN EDIT TAKE EFFECT. These values reach the process when the container is CREATED --
# docker-compose.yml hands deploy/.env.server through env_file: and MODEL_MAX_CONCURRENCY
# through environment: -- so editing the file changes nothing for a running container, and
# neither does docker restart. Use docker compose up -d --force-recreate, then prove it inside
# the process (docker exec enterprise-brain-backend-1 env | grep MODEL_). The image carries no
# .env of its own, so a variable nobody hands the container runs at its code default -- that is
# how a "configured" 4096 turned out to be an unset one (跟进单 §70, runbook P-8). Only a
# DEFAULT_* constant in app/common/model_budget.py needs an image rebuild, and only the server
# side needs a model reload: measured here, num_ctx 4096 -> 8192 cost load_s 0.001 -> 5.811 and
# prefill +3.3% on the same 2154-token prompt; nothing has been measured at 16384, so nobody
# should promise that hop is free.
MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS=4096

# --- row-level department scope (R17) -------------------------------------
# fail_closed (default): a data row whose department cannot be resolved stays invisible.
# legacy: the pre-R17 widening, kept only as a written-down rollback. Unset, empty or
# misspelled all mean fail_closed, because widening what a user can see has to be an
# edit somebody makes on purpose.
RBAC_ROW_DEPARTMENT_SCOPE=fail_closed

# --- edge ------------------------------------------------------------------
HTTP_PORT=80

# --- observability ---------------------------------------------------------
APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Shanghai
APP_DEFAULT_CURRENCY=CNY
LANGSMITH_TRACING=false

# --- retrieval tier --------------------------------------------------------
# Query-rewrite tier for document retrieval: full (default; one blocking chat-model
# round trip per search, unchanged behaviour) | adaptive (rewrite only when the
# question looks like it carries several intents) | fast (never rewrite, so one
# retrieval costs zero LLM round trips). Unset, empty or misspelled values fall
# back to full, because silently narrowing recall is a quality change.
# --- document activity prior (R46, shipped by R152; recalibration open as R153) ---
# Every answer that retrieves can nudge the order of its own candidates using a per-document count of
# 「this source was used / this source was wrong」, written through POST /api/v1/feedback/document.
# Storage is counts only: migrations/0011 has no text column, and the request body is refused (422) if
# it carries anything beyond filename and signal, so a user question cannot land in that table.
# on is the default, and that is also what an install that sets nothing gets. Rollback is an explicit
# off; 0 / false / no work too, while a value this file does not list is read as on, never as off -
# treating 「nobody configured it」 as 「configured off」 would retire the feature by typo.
# If the table is missing or PostgreSQL is unreachable the ranker keeps the order it had before R46
# (fail-open, and the reason is not lost: GET /api/v1/feedback/document reports prior_source and
# prior_reason, which is how 「no signal」 and 「no read」 stay distinguishable).
# Read the size of the nudge before trusting it. Since R153 the prior is stated in ranks, not in score:
# one net acceptance is worth half a rank and the hard bound is ACTIVITY_PRIOR_MAX_SHIFT_RANKS = 1,
# measured identical at leg widths 5, 12 and 40. Before that calibration one click could lift a hit from
# 5th to 1st in the k=5 leg this app ships. It can still swap the top two of a short leg, and there is no
# per-person throttle or cooldown (owner decision), so this switch stays an experiment, not a quality
# guarantee - off is still the conservative setting.
# The app reads this when the container is created, so changing it takes a recreate, not a reload.
RAG_ACTIVITY_PRIOR=on

RETRIEVAL_TIER=full
